{:answers {"connect-eventual-misinterp" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "**Flag: misinterpretation.** The note is wrong on two counts.\n\n1. **Convergence ≠ strong consistency.** Eventual consistency constrains only the *final state*: if updates stop, replicas converge. Strong consistency constrains *every operation*: each read must see the latest committed write, in a single agreed order. A system can converge and still never be strongly consistent — the moment a new write lands, replicas diverge again, and intermediate reads may see conflicting values with no agreed ordering. \"Eventually becomes strongly consistent\" is a category error: one is a guarantee about where state settles, the other about what each read observes.\n\n2. **\"A small delay\" is unsupported.** Eventual consistency gives *no bound* on the convergence window. Staleness can be arbitrarily long (network partitions, retry backlogs), and until convergence, reads can return not just stale values but *conflicting* values from different replicas.\n\n**Corrected note:** Eventual consistency guarantees only that replicas converge once updates stop; it places no bound on how long that takes and permits stale or conflicting reads in the meantime. It is a fundamentally weaker property than strong consistency — a liveness-of-state guarantee, not a per-read guarantee — not strong consistency with a delay.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "The note is wrong. Eventual consistency does **not** collapse into strong consistency with latency; the guarantees are structurally different.\n\n**Correction.** Strong consistency: every read returns the result of the most recent write. Eventual consistency: if writes halt, replicas *eventually* converge to the same state. Until convergence, reads may return stale values, divergent histories, or conflicting versions. There is **no time bound** on that convergence, so \"small delay\" is not merely imprecise—it misrepresents the model. A system can remain inconsistent indefinitely under continuous load, and applications must handle observable ambiguity rather than simply wait.\n\n**Short.** Strong consistency is a guarantee about every operation; eventual consistency is a liveness property about quiescence.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "Flagged: misinterprets. Correct connection uses the given concept.\n\n**Correction.** The note's error: convergence ≠ becoming strongly consistent, and \"small delay\" is wrong. Difference is semantic, not temporal.\n\n- Strong consistency (linearizability): every read returns the result of the latest completed write, per a single global order. Bound: operations are ordered *now.*\n- Eventual consistency: no linearizable order and no bound. If updates stop, replicas may reach some common state — but that state need not match what a strongly consistent system would return (e.g., conflicting concurrent writes may resolve to a value no strongly consistent system would have read), and reads *during* updates — including after your own write — may be stale. A session guarantee (`read-your-writes`) is an *added* guarantee, not implied.\n\nConverging-with-no-bound and updates-stopping-finite is therefore not strong consistency \"with delay\": it's a weaker contract (CAP: availability/partition tolerance in exchange for consistency). DBs advertise \"strong\" only with add-ons (quorum/linearizable reads, e.g. CockroachDB/ScyllaDB read-your-writes+monotonic via per-session fencing, or the few truly serializable ones) — not from eventual convergence.\n\n**Note's correct part:** eventual consistency does agree that *\"if updates stop, replicas converge.\"* **Wrong part:** it's not \"basically strong consistency,\" and the gap is not bounded delay.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "Flag: the note misinterprets the concept.\n\nCorrection: eventual consistency is **not** strong consistency with a small delay. It only guarantees replicas converge *if updates stop*; it gives no bound on when, so “small delay” is unsupported. Until convergence, reads may see stale or conflicting values. Strong consistency guarantees every read sees the latest committed write — a guarantee eventual consistency does not provide at any bounded moment.\n\nConnection: the note collapses “converges after quiescence” into “strongly consistent later,” but without a latency bound and with stale/conflicting reads allowed, the systems are not “basically the same thing.”\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "**Misinterpretation flagged.**\n\nThe note confuses convergence with strong consistency. Eventual consistency ≠ delayed strong consistency.\n\n**Correction:**\n\nEventual consistency guarantees replicas converge to the same state *if updates stop*—but:\n\n- **No timing bound**: \"eventually\" may be seconds, minutes, or indefinite under continuous writes\n- **Not strong consistency**: during convergence, reads may return stale data, see different values across replicas, or observe conflicting states that require resolution\n- **Weaker guarantees**: strong consistency ensures every read sees the latest write (linearizability); eventual consistency sacrifices this for availability and partition tolerance\n\n**Key difference**: Strong consistency is a *safety property* (correctness at every moment). Eventual consistency is a *liveness property* (convergence if quiescence happens). The latter trades immediate correctness for system availability—fundamentally different, not \"the same with delay.\"\n"}, "extract-security-note" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "Three concepts, all canonical; the NOTE paraphrases each rather than naming it (its phrasing is coinage, the concepts are not).\n\n| NOTE phrase | Concept | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"assume any single layer can be breached, so we require multiple independent checks\" | **Defense in depth** (layered security; no single point of failure) | Canonical |\n| \"every component gets only the access it needs\" | **Least privilege** (principle of least privilege / need-to-know) | Canonical |\n| \"nothing on the internal network is trusted just for being internal\" | **Zero trust** (\"never trust, always verify\"; no implicit trust from network location) | Canonical |\n\nCoinage: none. The NOTE introduces no new concept; it's a plain-language restatement of three textbook principles. Closest to a coined flavor is the third clause — \"trusted just for being internal\" inverts zero trust's usual framing (removing implicit perimeter trust) rather than citing the standard term.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "The NOTE paraphrases three canonical security concepts; its phrasing is non-standard/coined, the referents are established.\n\n| Concept | Status | NOTE's phrasing |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Defense in Depth** | canonical | \"any single layer can be breached, so we require multiple independent checks\" |\n| **Principle of Least Privilege** | canonical | \"every component gets only the access it needs\" |\n| **Zero Trust** (de-perimeterization / never trust, always verify) | canonical | \"nothing on the internal network is trusted just for being internal\" |\n\nThe NOTE does not introduce new security ideas; it restates these three canonical principles in plain language.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "NOTE parses into four standard principles. All phrasing is plain-language paraphrase; no coined terms.\n\n1. **Assumption of breach** — canonical (zero-trust tenet; NIST SP 800-207). \"any single layer can be breached.\"\n2. **Defense in depth** — canonical. \"multiple independent checks.\" The *independence* qualifier is a design property of good layering (no shared single failure mode), not a separate named principle.\n3. **Least privilege** — canonical. \"every component gets only the access it needs.\"\n4. **Zero trust / no implicit trust by network location** — canonical. \"nothing [internal] is trusted just for being internal.\"\n\nCoinage: **none**. \"Multiple independent checks\" is the least-codified wording in the NOTE, but it names no new concept — it restates defense in depth. If forced to label terms rather than concepts: every sentence maps 1:1 to an established named principle.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "Mapping each clause to the established concept:\n\n| NOTE phrasing | Established concept | Mark |\n|---|---|---|\n| “assume any single layer can be breached” | Assume breach / assume compromise; no single point of trust | Canonical |\n| “require multiple independent checks” | Defense in depth; the independence requirement ≈ separation of privilege (Saltzer & Schroeder, 1975) | Canonical |\n| “every component gets only the access it needs” | Least privilege | Canonical (Saltzer & Schroeder, 1975) |\n| “nothing on the internal network is trusted just for being internal” | Zero trust / deperimeterization; no implicit trust from network location | Canonical (NIST SP 800-207) |\n\nCoinage: none in the NOTE itself — the clauses are plain-language restatements of named principles, not new terms. “Zero Trust” began as a coined label but is now standardized.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "The NOTE invokes three established security concepts:\n\n1. **Defense in Depth** [canonical] — \"any single layer can be breached, so we require multiple independent checks\"\n\n2. **Least Privilege** [canonical] — \"every component gets only the access it needs\"\n\n3. **Zero Trust** [canonical] — \"nothing on the internal network is trusted just for being internal\"\n\nAll canonical; no coinage.\n"}, "connect-backpressure-correct" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "Confirmed. The note is an accurate description of **backpressure**: when the consumer's queue is full, the consumer stops reading from the socket. TCP's flow control (receive window shrinks as the buffer fills) then propagates that stall back to the producer, which blocks on send and slows down. Result: the system self-throttles instead of unboundedly buffering and exhausting memory.\n\nOne nuance, not a correction: the slowdown is indirect — the consumer doesn't signal the producer explicitly; the transport (TCP window, or blocked channel write in in-process queues) does. The note's causal chain (stop reading → producer slows) captures exactly this mechanism, so no correction needed.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "Confirmed. The note correctly describes backpressure.\n\nThe mechanism: consumer stops reading from the socket → TCP receive buffer fills → TCP window shrinks to zero → producer's `send()` blocks or fails with `EAGAIN` → producer slows. Flow control propagates upstream without explicit application coordination.\n\nYour queue acts as the boundary where consumption rate governs admission. Correct as stated.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "Confirmed. The note describes a concrete instance of backpressure: a full queue makes the consumer stop reading from the socket, the OS receive buffer fills, the TCP receive window closes, and the sender throttles (blocks its `send` or reduces its window). Pressure propagates upstream from consumer to producer, so the producer — not your memory — absorbs the imbalance.\n\nOne caveat: this bounds memory only up to the socket buffer plus in-flight data. It protects you insofar as all input arrives over that socket and the producer honors TCP flow control; non-socket producers or ignored window sizes bypass the mechanism.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "The note is a correct description of backpressure.\n\nMechanism: stopping socket reads stops draining the OS receive buffer / advertised TCP window. That window fills, TCP stops ACKing further data, and the producer's send buffer eventually blocks — so the producer slows instead of the consumer allocating unbounded memory. This is transport-level backpressure (TCP flow control), distinct from application-level signals like RSVP or request throttling.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "**Confirmed.** The note correctly describes backpressure via TCP flow control.\n\nWhen the consumer stops reading, the socket's receive buffer fills → TCP advertises zero window to sender → producer blocks/slows. The queue stays bounded; memory pressure shifts to the producer's send buffer instead of growing unbounded at the consumer.\n\nThis is standard TCP backpressure: consumer's processing rate naturally throttles the producer through protocol-level flow control.\n"}, "synth-bloom-no-sources" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "# Bloom filter\n\nA **Bloom filter** is a space-efficient probabilistic data structure that represents a set and supports membership queries. It may report false positives (an element not in the set may be reported as present), but never false negatives. Conceived by Burton H. Bloom (1970, *Communications of the ACM*).\n\n## Structure\n\n- A bit array of $m$ bits, initially all 0.\n- $k$ independent hash functions $h_1, \\dots, h_k$, each mapping an element to one of $m$ array positions.\n\n## Operations\n\n- **Insert**: set bits at positions $h_1(x), \\dots, h_k(x)$ to 1.\n- **Query**: check all $k$ positions; if any is 0, the element is definitely not in the set; if all are 1, it is *probably* present.\n- **Delete**: not supported in the basic form (clearing bits could affect other elements). Counting Bloom filters, replacing bits with small counters, permit deletion.\n\n## Error probability\n\nWith $n$ elements inserted, the false-positive probability is approximately:\n\n$$p \\approx \\left(1 - e^{-kn/m}\\right)^k$$\n\nThe optimal number of hash functions, minimizing $p$ for fixed $m$ and $n$:\n\n$$k = \\frac{m}{n}\\ln 2$$\n\ngiving $p \\approx (0.6185)^{m/n}$. Required bits per element for a target false-positive rate $p$: $m/n \\approx -1.44 \\log_2 p$ (≈ 9.6 bits/element for $p = 1\\%$).\n\n## Properties\n\n- **No false negatives**: querying an inserted element always returns positive (deterministic, assuming hash outputs unchanged).\n- **Union/intersection**: bit-wise OR of two filters (same $m$, $k$) yields a filter for the set union; bit-wise AND approximates intersection (may raise false positives).\n- **Cannot enumerate** stored elements or count them exactly; cannot retrieve the elements themselves.\n- **Size fixed at creation**; cannot grow without rebuilding ( scalable variants: scalable Bloom filters, blocked Bloom filters).\n- Standard construction is not cryptographically secure; adversarial inputs can force high false-positive rates ( hardening approaches exist).\n\n## Variants\n\nCounting Bloom filter (deletion), scalable Bloom filter (growth), blocked Bloom filter (cache locality), cuckoo filter (deletion + lower space at small $p$), xor filter / ribbon filter (static sets, smaller footprint), Bloomier filter (approximate maps, not just sets).\n\n## Applications\n\n- Databases and storage: avoid disk lookups for absent keys (historically cited in early use).\n- Web caching and networking: one-time-seen URLs, packet inspection, duplicate suppression.\n- Distributed systems: anti-entropy, sync pruning (e.g., in Cassandra and HBase-style key stores), Bitcoin SPV clients (BIP-37, now discouraged).\n- Spell checkers, malicious-URL blacklists, deduplication systems.\n\n## Trade-offs\n\nSpace far below exact-set representations (e.g., hash tables, sorted arrays) at equal query cost — $O(k)$ probes with constant-time insert/query — but probabilistic answers, no deletion (basic form), and irrecoverable false positives once bits saturate. Fill ratio must be bounded; as $m/n$ grows worse, $p$ degrades rapidly.\n\n---\n*Sources: attribute the concept to Bloom's 1970 CACM article (\"Space/Time Trade-offs in Hash Coding with Allowable Errors\"); formulas and variant descriptions are standard textbook material. No page numbers or specific bibliographic details cited.*\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "**Bloom filter**\n\nA **Bloom filter** is a space-efficient probabilistic data structure, conceived by Burton Howard Bloom in 1970, that tests whether an element is a member of a set. Queries may yield false positives, but never false negatives.\n\n**Mechanism**  \nThe structure comprises a bit array of $m$ bits, initially all set to 0, and $k$ independent hash functions. To add an element, it is hashed by the $k$ functions to determine $k$ indices, and the corresponding bits are set to 1. To query an element, the same $k$ indices are checked; if any bit is 0, the element is definitely absent. If all bits are 1, the element is possibly present (the bits may have been set by other inserted elements).\n\n**Properties**  \n- Insertion and query time complexity is $O(k)$, effectively constant for fixed $k$.  \n- Space usage is substantially lower than storing elements explicitly.  \n- Standard Bloom filters do not support deletion.  \n- For $n$ inserted elements, the false positive probability is approximately  \n  $$p \\approx \\left(1 - e^{-kn/m}\\right)^k,$$  \n  minimized when $k = \\frac{m}{n}\\ln 2$.\n\n**Variants**  \nA **counting Bloom filter** replaces bits with counters to allow deletion at increased space cost.\n\n**Applications**  \nBloom filters reduce expensive disk or network lookups in databases, caches, and distributed systems; they are also used in network routing and in simplified payment verification for cryptocurrencies.\n\n**See also**  \n- Hash function  \n- Cuckoo filter  \n- Set membership problem  \n\n**References**  \n- Bloom, B. H. (1970). \"Space/Time Trade-offs in Hash Coding with Allowable Errors\". *Communications of the ACM*.  \n- Mitzenmacher, M., & Upfal, E. (2005). *Probability and Computing: Randomized Algorithms and Probabilistic Analysis*. Cambridge University Press.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "# Bloom filter\n\nProbabilistic space-efficient data structure for membership queries. Tests whether an element belongs to a set; may report a **false positive** (element not in set reported present) but **never a false negative** (inserted elements are always reported present). Invented by Burton Howard Bloom and published in 1970.\n\n## Representation\n\n- A bit array of length *m*, initially all zeros.\n- *k* independent hash functions, each mapping an element to one of the *m* positions.\n- **Insert:** apply all *k* hashes; set each resulting bit to 1.\n- **Query:** apply all *k* hashes; if any bit is 0, the element is definitely absent; if all are 1, the element is probably present.\n- Elements cannot be listed or removed with the standard design; deletion requires a variant (e.g., counting Bloom filter, which stores small counters instead of single bits).\n\n## Error and sizing\n\nFalse-positive probability for *n* inserted elements approximates\n\n$$\\left(1 - e^{-kn/m}\\right)^k$$\n\nand is minimized when the number of hash functions is near\n\n$$k = \\frac{m}{n}\\ln 2$$\n\ni.e., roughly 6.9 bits per element for a 1% false-positive rate. Unlike hash tables, a Bloom filter never stores the elements themselves, so it stays compact regardless of element size.\n\n## Behavior and limits\n\n- No false negatives; deterministic for the query side.\n- Union and intersection of sets can be approximated by bitwise OR/AND of filters sharing size and hash functions.\n- Multiplicative composition of *k* operations makes position computation costlier than a single hash lookup.\n- Naive bloom filter cannot count occurrences or remove exact items without deletion support.\n\n## Applications\n\nSits where a cheap membership oracle saves expensive lookups and a small false-positive error is acceptable:\n\n- **Databases:** avoid disk page fetches for absent keys (early use in spell checkers and dictionary lookups); optional bloom filter blocks in LevelDB/RocksDB, and compaction/read paths in Cassandra, HBase, and others.\n- **Caches and web:** avoid cache misses being forwarded to origin; CDN no-cache key checks are a classic case.\n- **Crawlers and URL deduplication:** skip already-visited pages without keeping a full URL table.\n- **Network / blockchain:** low-state membership checks in traffic classification; Bitcoin simplified payment verification uses bloom-filtered peer queries (now deprecated in favor of other protocols in many clients).\n- **Anti-abuse:** Chrome Safe Browsing ships a compact local list of malicious URL hashes sharded across servers.\n\n## Related structures\n\nCuckoo filters, quotient filters, and counting Bloom filters trade different guarantees (deletion, enumeration, or smaller keys) at varying memory cost; scalable Bloom filters grow as the element count grows.\n\n## Citations note\n\nThis card is written from established, well-known background knowledge. No external sources were supplied or independently consulted for this writing, and no specific publications, page numbers, or URLs are attributed here beyond the widely cited 1970 origin; verification of precise claims against a primary source is left to the reader.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "# Bloom filter\n\nA **Bloom filter** is a space-efficient probabilistic data structure used to test whether an element is a member of a set. It reports **\"possibly in set\"** or **\"definitely not in set.\"** False positives are possible, but false negatives are not.\n\n## Structure\n\n- An array of `m` bits, initially all zero.\n- `k` independent hash functions, each mapping an element to a bit position in `[0, m−1]`.\n\n## Operations\n\n- **Insert(x)** — set the bits at positions `h₁(x), …, hₖ(x)` to 1.\n- **Query(x)** — test the bits at `h₁(x), …, hₖ(x)`:\n  - any bit is 0 → `x` is **definitely not** in the set.\n  - all bits are 1 → `x` is **possibly** in the set (or a false positive).\n\n## Parameters\n\nGiven `n` expected elements and target false-positive rate `p`:\n\n- Minimum bit count: `m = −(n ln p) / (ln 2)²`\n- Optimal hash-function count: `k = (m / n) ln 2`\n- False-positive probability: `(1 − e^(−kn/m))^k`; at optimal `k`, approximately `0.6185^(m/n)`\n\n## Properties\n\n- Insert and query each require `O(k)` time, independent of set size.\n- Guarantees no false negatives.\n- Does not store the elements themselves; the stored set cannot be enumerated.\n\n## Variants\n\n- **Counting Bloom filter** — uses counters instead of bits to support deletion.\n- **Scalable / partitioned Bloom filter** — dynamically grows when expected capacity is unknown.\n- **Cuckoo filter** — supports deletion and often lower false-positive rates at the same size.\n\n## Applications\n\n- Databases and caches to avoid expensive lookups.\n- Network routers and content-addressable systems.\n- Web caching and spell checkers.\n- Distributed-system membership and privacy-preserving filters.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- Standard version does not support element deletion.\n- Stored elements cannot be recovered or listed.\n- False-positive rate grows as the filter approaches capacity.\n- Requires high-quality, independent hash functions.\n\n## Provenance\n\nIntroduced by Burton H. Bloom in 1970: \"Space/time trade-offs in hash coding with allowable errors,\" *Communications of the ACM*, 13(7): 422–426.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# Bloom Filter\n\nA **Bloom filter** is a space-efficient probabilistic data structure used to test whether an element is a member of a set. It was proposed by Burton Howard Bloom in 1970.\n\n## Mechanism\n\nA Bloom filter uses a bit array of *m* bits, initially all set to 0, and *k* independent hash functions that map elements to positions in the array. To add an element, the element is hashed by each of the *k* functions, and the corresponding bits are set to 1. To query membership, the element is hashed again; if all *k* corresponding bits are 1, the element is *possibly* in the set. If any bit is 0, the element is *definitely not* in the set.\n\n## Properties\n\n- **Space efficiency**: Bloom filters use significantly less space than storing the actual elements, independent of element size or cardinality (up to the filter's capacity).\n- **False positives**: Membership queries may incorrectly return positive (the bit positions were set by other elements). The false positive rate depends on *m*, *n* (number of inserted elements), and *k*.\n- **No false negatives**: If an element was inserted, the query will always return positive.\n- **No deletions**: Standard Bloom filters do not support element removal (deletion would risk false negatives). Counting Bloom filters use counters instead of bits to enable deletion.\n- **Time complexity**: Insertion and query operations are *O(k)*.\n\n## Optimal Parameters\n\nThe false positive probability *p* is minimized when *k* = (*m*/*n*) ln 2. The optimal bit array size is *m* = −(*n* ln *p*) / (ln 2)².\n\n## Applications\n\n- Database systems (query optimization, avoiding disk lookups)\n- Network routers (packet filtering, cache digests)\n- Web browsers (malicious URL blocking)\n- Distributed systems (reducing inter-node communication)\n- Spell checkers\n\n## Variants\n\nNotable extensions include **counting Bloom filters** (support deletion), **scalable Bloom filters** (dynamic capacity), and **cuckoo filters** (alternative structure with deletion support and better space efficiency at low false positive rates).\n"}, "synth-idempotency" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "# Idempotency (computing/APIs)\n\n**Idempotency** is the property of an operation whereby performing it multiple times has the same effect as performing it once. [1]\n\nIn the context of HTTP, the methods **GET**, **HEAD**, **PUT**, and **DELETE** are idempotent, while **POST** is not. A practical consequence is that idempotent methods can be retried safely after a failure. [2]\n\n## References\n\n1. Wikipedia — definition of idempotent operation.\n2. MDN (HTTP) — idempotent HTTP methods and retry safety.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "**Idempotency (computing/APIs)**\n\nAn operation is idempotent if performing it multiple times has the same effect as performing it once[1]. In HTTP, GET, HEAD, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent; POST is not. Idempotent methods can be retried safely after a failure[2].\n\n**Sources**\n[1] Wikipedia\n[2] MDN, HTTP\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "# Idempotency (computing/APIs)\n\n**Idempotency** is a property of an operation whereby performing it multiple times has the same effect as performing it once [1].\n\nIn HTTP, the methods GET, HEAD, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent; POST is not [2]. Because their repeated execution does not change the resulting effect, idempotent methods can be retried safely after a failure [2].\n\n**References**  \n[1] Wikipedia — Idempotence: \"An operation is idempotent if performing it multiple times has the same effect as performing it once.\"  \n[2] MDN Web Docs (HTTP): \"GET, HEAD, PUT and DELETE are idempotent; POST is not. Idempotent methods can be retried safely after a failure.\"\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "# Idempotency (computing/APIs)\n\nAn operation is **idempotent** if performing it multiple times has the same effect as performing it once.<sup>[1]</sup>\n\n- In HTTP, **GET, HEAD, PUT, and DELETE** are idempotent; **POST** is not.<sup>[2]</sup>\n- Idempotent methods can be retried safely after a failure.<sup>[2]</sup>\n\n## Sources\n1. Wikipedia — \"An operation is idempotent if performing it multiple times has the same effect as performing it once.\"\n2. MDN (HTTP) — \"GET, HEAD, PUT and DELETE are idempotent; POST is not. Idempotent methods can be retried safely after a failure.\"\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# Idempotency (computing/APIs)\n\nAn operation is **idempotent** if performing it multiple times has the same effect as performing it once.[1]\n\nIn HTTP, GET, HEAD, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent methods; POST is not.[2] Idempotent methods can be retried safely after a failure.[2]\n\n---\n\n**Sources:**\n1. Wikipedia\n2. MDN, HTTP\n"}, "extract-coinage-trap" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "The NOTE is inline in your message — no file read needed.\n\n**Author's coined term**\n- **\"context capsule\"** — explicitly flagged as the author's own (\"my term\"). Not established terminology. It describes an established *pattern* (see below), but the name itself is coined and must not be cited as a known concept.\n\n**Well-established concepts it invokes**\n- **Cascading failure / fault propagation** — \"a single bug can't cascade\" is the standard concept from fault-tolerant systems design (failure containment, preventing error propagation).\n- **Blast radius** — established term in software engineering and security/incident-response discourse for the scope of damage a failure or compromise can cause.\n- **Isolation / sandboxing with minimal inputs** — \"carries only the inputs it needs\" invokes the principle of least privilege and hermetic/isolated execution contexts.\n- **Manifest-driven construction** — \"manifest\" is canonical (build systems, package managers, deployment tooling all use it for a declarative input list).\n- **Reproducibility / determinism** — \"rebuilt from a manifest… identical each time\" invokes reproducible builds / deterministic execution: same inputs → same output, no ambient state.\n\nOne caution: the NOTE's *claim* that capsule rebuild guarantees identity each run is an assertion, not something the established concepts guarantee by themselves — manifests give determinism only if the manifest fully pins its inputs (no floating versions, no ambient state). As written, it's plausible but unverified.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "Established concepts the NOTE invokes:\n\n| Concept | Status | Why invoked |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Blast radius** | Canonical-established | Scope of impact when a component fails; standard in reliability engineering and DevOps. |\n| **Manifest** | Canonical-established | Declarative inventory of dependencies/inputs used to reconstruct an environment or artifact deterministically. |\n| **Deterministic/reproducible build** | Canonical-established | Rebuilding from the same manifest yields identical state every time. |\n| **Fault isolation / failure containment** | Canonical-established | Restricting inputs/scope so a bug in one unit cannot cascade to others. |\n| **Encapsulation / minimal dependency** | Canonical-established | Bundling only necessary inputs to limit coupling and external visibility. |\n\nAuthor’s coined term:\n\n| Term | Status | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Context capsule** | Coined | Explicitly labeled “(my term)” by the author. |\n\nThe NOTE wraps these established ideas into the author’s “context capsule” formulation, but the underlying mechanics—manifest-driven reproducibility, fault isolation, and blast-radius containment—are pre-existing engineering concepts.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "NOTE text is inline; no file to open. Concepts invoked:\n\n**Canonical-established**\n- **Fault isolation / blast radius** — isolating failure so a bug can't cascade is standard reliability engineering (\"blast radius\" is established jargon in incident/reliability practice).\n- **Least privilege / minimal interface** — \"carries only the inputs it needs\" restates Saltzer & Schroeder's principle of least privilege applied to task boundaries.\n- **Deterministic / reproducible construction** — \"identical each time\" from the same manifest is the reproducible-builds concept (reproducible builds, Nix derivations, declarative config).\n- **Manifest-driven rebuild** — reconstructing state from a declarative manifest is established practice (Dockerfile, Kubernetes manifests, Nix, Terraform state→apply).\n- **Isolation boundary** — the unit of encapsulation around a task (generic, established framing).\n\n**Author's coined term**\n- **\"context capsule\"** — the author's own term (\"my term\"). Not established usage; treat it as a label, not a recognized concept.\n\nEverything the capsule *does* decomposes into the established concepts above. One gap worth naming: scoping inputs doesn't by itself guarantee \"identical each time\" — determinism also requires pinning *external* state (versions, environment, randomness, time). Unstated whether the manifest covers that; if not, the \"identical\" claim is an aspiration, not an invariant.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "## Concepts in the NOTE\n\n| Concept | Status | Evidence / canonical name |\n|---|---|---|\n| Context capsule | **Coined** | Author flags it: \"my term\". No established meaning; it's their label for the overall scheme. |\n| Task-isolated input set — only the inputs a task needs | **Canonical** | Least privilege / minimal context / dependency injection. Established practice, not the author's invention. |\n| Containing failure so one bug doesn't spread | **Canonical** | Fault isolation / containment boundaries (reliability and security engineering). |\n| Blast radius | **Canonical** | Standard reliability-engineering metaphor: *limit the blast radius* / blast-radius reduction. |\n| Failure cascading | **Canonical** | Cascade failure — established term in distributed systems and safety engineering. |\n| Manifest as declarative record of what the capsule contains | **Canonical** | Build manifest / manifest file — standard in build and release tooling (e.g., package manifests, lockfiles). |\n| Rebuilding identically from the manifest every run | **Canonical** | Reproducible / deterministic build; idempotent reconstruction. Established concepts, jointly.\n\n**Only the coined term:** \"context capsule.\" Everything it *relies on* — least-privilege inputs, fault isolation, blast-radius containment, manifest-driven reproducibility — is established engineering vocabulary.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# Well-Established Concepts\n\n- **Encapsulation/isolation** — containing dependencies/state\n- **Fault isolation** — preventing bugs from spreading\n- **Blast radius** — scope of failure impact (common in incident response, infrastructure)\n- **Cascading failures** — one failure triggering others\n- **Reproducible builds/deterministic execution** — identical output from identical input\n- **Manifest-driven construction** — building from declarative specification\n\n# Author's Coined Terms\n\n- **\"context capsule\"** — explicitly marked \"(my term)\"\n\nThe note describes a pattern combining fault isolation, reproducible builds, and manifest-based construction. The only coined term is \"context capsule\"; the rest map to established software engineering concepts.\n"}, "synth-two-phase-commit" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "# Two-phase commit (2PC)\n\nA distributed transaction protocol in which a coordinator asks all participants to **prepare**; if all participants vote yes, the coordinator instructs them to **commit** — otherwise it instructs them to **abort** [1].\n\n2PC is a **blocking** protocol: if the coordinator fails after the prepare phase, participants can remain stuck holding locks until the coordinator recovers [2].\n\n**Sources**\n1. SOURCE 1 (protocol definition)\n2. SOURCE 2 (blocking failure mode)\n\n**Coverage note:** sources provide only the voting/decision flow and one failure mode; no details available on phase names, timeouts, or variants (e.g., 3PC).\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "**Two-phase commit (2PC)** is a distributed transaction protocol in which a coordinator asks all participants to prepare; if all vote yes, the coordinator tells them to commit, otherwise it tells them to abort [1]. It is a blocking protocol: if the coordinator fails after prepare, participants can be stuck holding locks until it recovers [2].\n\n---\n\n**Sources**\n\n[1] Source 1  \n[2] Source 2\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "# Two-phase commit (2PC)\n\nIn two-phase commit (2PC), a coordinator asks all participants to prepare; if all vote yes, it tells them to commit; otherwise it tells them to abort [1].\n\n2PC is a blocking protocol: if the coordinator fails after the prepare phase, participants can remain stuck holding locks until the coordinator recovers [2].\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "## Two-phase commit (2PC)\n\nA distributed transaction protocol in which a coordinator asks all participants to prepare; if all vote yes, the coordinator tells them to commit, otherwise to abort [1].\n\nIt is a blocking protocol: if the coordinator fails after the prepare phase, participants can be stuck holding locks until it recovers [2].\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# Two-Phase Commit (2PC)\n\n**Two-phase commit** is a distributed protocol for coordinating atomic transactions across multiple participants [1].\n\n## Protocol\n\nA coordinator manages the process in two phases [1]:\n1. **Prepare phase**: The coordinator asks all participants to prepare; participants vote yes or no\n2. **Commit phase**: If all participants vote yes, the coordinator instructs them to commit; otherwise, it instructs them to abort\n\n## Limitations\n\n2PC is a **blocking protocol**: if the coordinator fails after the prepare phase, participants can remain stuck holding locks until the coordinator recovers [2]. This represents a significant availability tradeoff.\n\n---\n**Sources**: [1] SOURCE 1; [2] SOURCE 2\n"}, "synth-vector-clock" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "# Vector clock\n\nA **vector clock** is a data structure for ordering events in a distributed system. It consists of a vector of per-node counters, one entry per node. Each node increments its own entry when an event occurs, and on receiving a message, merges the incoming clock with its own by element-wise maximum [1].\n\nComparison of two vectors yields a partial order over events: it determines whether one event **happened-before** another, or whether the two events are **concurrent** (i.e., causally unordered) [2].\n\n## References\n\n1. Source 1 — definition of vector clock structure and update rules.\n2. Source 2 — comparison semantics: happened-before and concurrency detection.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "**Vector clock** — a data structure in distributed systems for capturing event ordering. It is maintained as a vector of per-node counters; each node increments its own entry when an event occurs, and merges incoming vectors by taking the element-wise maximum upon message receipt[1]. Comparison of two vectors establishes a partial order, enabling determination of whether one event happened-before another or whether the events are concurrent[2].\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "A vector clock is a vector of per-node counters; each node increments its own entry on an event and merges by element-wise maximum on message receipt (SOURCE 1). Comparing vectors yields a partial order: it detects whether one event happened-before another or whether they are concurrent (SOURCE 2).\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "**Vector clock** — a vector of per-node counters; each node increments its own entry on an event and merges by element-wise maximum on message receipt [SOURCE 1]. Comparing vectors yields a partial order: it detects whether one event happened-before another or whether they are concurrent [SOURCE 2].\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# Vector Clock\n\nA **vector clock** is a vector of per-node counters used in distributed systems.[1] Each node increments its own entry on an event and merges received vectors by element-wise maximum on message receipt.[1]\n\nVector comparison establishes a partial order over events: it determines whether one event happened-before another or whether events are concurrent.[2]\n\n---\n**References**  \n[1] SOURCE 1  \n[2] SOURCE 2\n"}, "synth-cap-bad-source" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "# CAP theorem\n\n**Term:** CAP theorem (a.k.a. Brewer's theorem)\n\n**Definition:** A distributed system that stores data across multiple networked nodes cannot simultaneously guarantee all three of the following:\n\n- **Consistency (C):** every read receives the most recent write (linearizability, in the formal statement).\n- **Availability (A):** every request receives a (non-error) response, without a guarantee that it reflects the latest write.\n- **Partition tolerance (P):** the system continues to operate despite messages being dropped or delayed between nodes (network partition).\n\n**Precise statement:** When a partition occurs, the system must sacrifice either consistency (serving possibly stale data to remain available) or availability (refusing requests until the partition heals). Outside of partitions, the C–A tradeoff largely disappears. First proposed by Eric Brewer (PODC keynote, 2000); proven for a specific model by Gilbert and Lynch (2002).\n\n**Common misconception — flagged in the source:** The source states you \"pick any two.\" This is inaccurate:\n\n1. **Partition tolerance is not optional.** Partitions are a property of the network, not a design choice — in any real distributed system, P must be held, since partitions *will* happen. (Coda Hale, \"You Can't Sacrifice Partition Tolerance,\" 2010; Brewer, \"CAP Twelve Years Later,\" 2012.)\n2. **The choice is only forced during a partition.** A system can be both consistent and available when the network is healthy; CAP constrains behavior *during* partitions, not at all times.\n3. **The theorem permits more nuance than a binary pick.** Post-2012 refinement (e.g., PACELC) notes tradeoffs exist even without partitions (latency vs. consistency), and systems may choose per-operation or per-datacenter.\n\n**Source note:** Source is a blog post asserting the \"pick any two\" framing; that framing is rejected by the theorem's own originator and by the standard literature. The card above corrects rather than repeats it, per the accuracy requirement.\n\n**See also:** PACELC, linearizability, eventual consistency, Byzantine fault tolerance.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "**CAP theorem** (also known as **Brewer’s theorem**)\n\n**Statement:** A distributed data store cannot simultaneously guarantee all three of the following properties:\n*   **Consistency** — Every read receives the most recent write or an error.\n*   **Availability** — Every request receives a non-error response, without guarantee that it contains the most recent write.\n*   **Partition tolerance** — The system continues to operate despite an arbitrary number of messages being dropped (or delayed) by the network between nodes.\n\n**Clarification:** Although the theorem is often popularly summarized as “a distributed system can only ever have two of Consistency, Availability, and Partition tolerance, so you just pick any two,” this framing is inaccurate. The formal result proven by Gilbert and Lynch (2002) states specifically that **in the presence of a network partition**, a system must choose between consistency and availability. Partition tolerance is not an optional property in a distributed system operating over an asynchronous network; because network failures are inevitable, the real design trade-off is deciding between consistency and availability *when a partition occurs*, not selecting any two of three in the abstract.\n\n**History:** Conjectured by Eric Brewer in 2000; formally proven in 2002 by Seth Gilbert and Nancy Lynch.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "# CAP theorem — wiki card\n\n**Definition.** CAP theorem (Brewer's theorem): for a distributed data store whose nodes can be isolated from each other by a network partition, it is impossible to guarantee both **Consistency** and **Availability** during such a partition. Consistency = every read returns the most recent write (linearizability); Availability = every request received by a non-failing node returns a response; Partition tolerance = the system must keep operating correctly despite arbitrary message loss between nodes.\n\n**Origin and status.** Proposed by Eric Brewer as a conjecture (PODC 2000 keynote); proved by Seth Gilbert and Nancy Lynch (2002), showing no algorithm can be both atomic (consistent) and available in a network that may drop messages.\n\n**Correct reading.** Partition tolerance is not an opt-in property — partitions cannot be prevented, so a distributed system must cope with them. The real choice occurs *during* a partition: favor Consistency and refuse to serve possibly-stale data (CP, e.g., ZooKeeper, HBase) or keep answering with possibly-stale data (AP, e.g., Dynamo, Cassandra). When no partition occurs, a system can deliver both consistency and availability.\n\n**Source accuracy.** The supplied source — \"CAP says a distributed system can only ever have two of Consistency, Availability, and Partition tolerance, so you just pick any two\" — is the popular two-out-of-three slogan, and taken literally it is inaccurate:\n\n- Partition tolerance isn't one of three options to choose from; any realistic distributed system must tolerate partitions to exist at all.\n- Consistency and availability are both achievable when there is no partition; CAP only forces the trade-off while a partition is in progress. No system is stuck with exactly two of the three forever.\n\nSo the slogan is a rough, misleading shorthand; the precise claim is: *during a network partition, choose between consistency and availability.*\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "# CAP theorem\n\n## Definition\nThe **CAP theorem** (also **Brewer's theorem**) states that a distributed data store can simultaneously provide at most **two** of three guarantees:\n\n- **Consistency (C):** every read returns the most recent write, or an error.\n- **Availability (A):** every request receives a non-error response, but without any guarantee that it contains the most recent write.\n- **Partition tolerance (P):** the system continues to operate despite arbitrary message loss, delay, or network failure between nodes.\n\n## Accurate formulation\nCAP is **not** \"pick any two.\" The supplied source is inaccurate.\n\nIn a networked distributed system, **network partitions are possible and cannot be avoided**. Therefore **partition tolerance is effectively mandatory**: a system that loses P stops working whenever the network fails.\n\nWhen a partition occurs, the system must choose:\n\n- **CP** — remain consistent at the cost of availability: some requests fail or time out rather than return stale data.\n- **AP** — remain available at the cost of consistency: some requests may return stale data.\n\nThus the real trade-off is **C versus A during a partition**, not a symmetric choice among all three properties.\n\n## Corrections to the source claim\n\n> \"CAP says a distributed system can only ever have two of Consistency, Availability, and Partition tolerance, so you just pick any two.\"\n\nProblems with this statement:\n\n1. **\"Pick any two\" is wrong.** Partition tolerance is not optional in a real distributed system.\n2. **The trade-off is not symmetric.** Choosing \"CA without P\" is impossible in a practical network, because partitions will occur regardless of whether the system is designed to tolerate them.\n3. **\"Only ever have two\" is misleading.** A CP or AP system may deliver both consistency and availability during normal operation with no partition; the forced choice appears only while a partition is active.\n4. **The properties are not binary.** Consistency and availability are often matters of degree, not absolute on/off guarantees.\n\n## Key point\nCAP says: **when a network partition happens, pick consistency or availability** — not any two of three in general.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# CAP Theorem\n\n**Misconception in source**: The provided source contains the common misstatement that CAP lets you \"pick any two\" of Consistency, Availability, and Partition tolerance.\n\n**Accurate statement**: The CAP theorem states that when a network **partition** occurs in a distributed system, you must choose between **Consistency** and **Availability**. Partition tolerance is not optional—network partitions are inevitable failures that will occur.\n\n## Terms\n\n- **Consistency**: Every read receives the most recent write or an error\n- **Availability**: Every request receives a non-error response (without guarantee of most recent write)\n- **Partition tolerance**: The system continues operating despite network partitions (arbitrary message loss between nodes)\n\n## What CAP Actually Says\n\nDuring a network partition, a distributed system faces a forced choice:\n- **CP**: Reject requests to maintain consistency (sacrifice availability)\n- **AP**: Serve requests with potentially stale data (sacrifice consistency)\n\nWhen the network is healthy (no partition), systems can provide both consistency and availability. The theorem only applies when partitions occur.\n"}, "extract-distributed-note" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "All concepts invoked are canonical; the note coins no new terms. Two concepts are invoked by description rather than by their standard names — flagged below.\n\n| Concept | Trigger text in note | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sharding (data partitioning) | \"shards writes across nodes\" | Canonical term, used by name |\n| Replication / asynchronous replication | \"replicas\", \"returns before all replicas ack\" | Canonical; async variant implied by returning pre-ack |\n| Acknowledgment / quorum-style commit | \"ack\" | Canonical shorthand |\n| Eventual consistency / stale read | \"a read right after a write can be stale\" | Canonical concept, **invoked without its name**; also a read-your-writes violation |\n| At-least-once delivery | \"Clients retry on timeout\" | Canonical; retry-on-timeout is the textbook cause |\n| Idempotency | \"handlers must tolerate duplicates\" | Canonical concept, **invoked without its name** — \"tolerate duplicates\" is the plain-language definition of idempotent handlers |\n| Cache invalidation (invalidate-on-write policy) | \"cache reads and invalidate on write\" | Canonical caching pattern, stated by mechanism |\n\nNo coinage: every phrase maps 1:1 onto standard distributed-systems vocabulary. The only \"new\" surface is descriptive phrasing (\"tolerate duplicates\", \"stale\"), not terminology — if this note were spec text, I'd rewrite those two to the canonical terms (idempotent, eventually consistent) to make the guarantees searchable and unambiguous.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "Concepts invoked in the NOTE:\n\n- **Sharding** (canonical): partitioning writes across nodes.\n- **Asynchronous replication** (canonical): returning before all replicas acknowledge.\n- **Stale reads / read-your-writes violation** (canonical): observable lag between write and read visibility.\n- **Timeout and retry** (canonical): client-side resending on presumed failure.\n- **Idempotency** (canonical): handler tolerance for duplicate requests.\n- **Read caching with write-invalidation** (canonical): populating cache on read, explicitly invalidating on write to maintain coherency.\n\nAll concepts are canonical; the NOTE contains no apparent coinages.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "Concepts invoked by the NOTE, mapped to standard terms:\n\n1. **Sharding** — \"shards writes across nodes.\" Canonical: sharding / horizontal partitioning. Note phrasing: descriptive coinage, standard underlying concept. Well-established.\n\n2. **Write ack before quorum / weak durability** — \"returns before all replicas ack.\" Canonical form: non-quorum write, asynchronous replication, low write concern (e.g., MongoDB `w:1`, Cassandra `CL=ONE`). Concept canonical; tradeoff is durability vs latency.\n\n3. **Eventual consistency / replication lag** — \"read right after a write can be stale.\" The specific canonical guarantee being absent: **read-your-writes (RYW) consistency**. Both \"stale read\" and \"replication lag\" are standard phrasings.\n\n4. **At-least-once delivery** — \"clients retry on timeout.\" Canonical term (vs. at-most-once, exactly-once). Retry exhausting, generating duplicates.\n\n5. **Idempotency** — \"handlers must tolerate duplicates.\" Canonical concept: duplicate-tolerant handlers (idempotent operations or dedup keys), the standard complement to at-least-once.\n\n6. **Cache invalidation on write** — \"cache reads and invalidate on write.\" Canonical classic: write-through invalidation; tradeoff with stale-cache reads.\n\nAlso implied but not named: **quorum** (why \"all replicas\" vs a majority), **durability**, and the stale-vs-stale-read distinction.\n\nClassification:\n\n- Terminologically **canonical** (standard labels): sharding, at-least-once, idempotency, cache invalidation, read-your-writes, eventual consistency, replication lag.\n- **Coinage/descriptive paraphrase** in the NOTE (wrapping canonical concepts): \"returns before all replicas ack\" (≈ non-quorum write / weak durability), \"read right after a write can be stale\" (≈ absence of RYW, replication lag), \"tolerate duplicates\" (≈ idempotency). No genuine novel terms coined; the NOTE is a plain-language restatement of well-established distributed-systems concepts.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "Canonical concepts invoked, with the note’s informal phrase vs. the standard name:\n\n| Note phrase | Concept | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| shards writes across nodes | sharding / horizontal partitioning | canonical |\n| returns before all replicas ack | asynchronous replication / unacknowledged writes (replication lag) | canonical |\n| a read right after a write can be stale | read-after-write inconsistency / missing read-your-writes guarantee | canonical |\n| Clients retry on timeout | retry semantics / at-least-once delivery | canonical |\n| handlers must tolerate duplicates | idempotency | canonical |\n| cache reads and invalidate on write | cache-aside / cache invalidation | canonical |\n\nCoinage: none. The note’s wording is informal paraphrase; every underlying concept is a standard distributed-systems term.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "**Canonical concepts:**\n\n1. **Sharding** — partitioning data across nodes\n2. **Replication** / **replicas** — data copies for availability/durability\n3. **Acknowledgment (ack)** — confirming operation receipt/completion\n4. **Eventual consistency** — replicas converge over time; reads may lag writes\n5. **Stale reads** — reading outdated data\n6. **Timeout** — operation deadline\n7. **Retry** — re-attempting failed/unacknowledged operations\n8. **At-least-once delivery** — retries produce duplicate requests\n9. **Idempotence** — duplicate operations produce same result as single execution\n10. **Caching** — storing computed/retrieved data for reuse\n11. **Cache invalidation** — removing/marking cached data obsolete on writes\n\n**Coinages:**\n\nNone. All terms map to standard distributed systems, networking, or caching literature.\n\n**Implied but unnamed:**\n\n- **Asynchronous replication** — not waiting for replica acks before returning\n- **CAP theorem tradeoff** — availability over consistency (C sacrificed for A/P)\n"}, "synth-consistent-hashing" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "# Consistent hashing\n\n**Consistent hashing** is a key-distribution technique in distributed systems in which keys and nodes are mapped onto a ring; each key is owned by the next node clockwise from its position on the ring [1].\n\n## Properties\n\nWhen a node is added or removed, only about $K/n$ keys are remapped (where $K$ is the number of keys and $n$ the number of nodes), rather than nearly all keys as in naive modular hashing [2]. The technique employs **virtual nodes** — multiple ring positions per physical node — to improve key distribution balance [2].\n\n---\n\n**Sources**\n1. \"Consistent hashing maps keys and nodes onto a ring; a key is owned by the next node clockwise.\"\n2. \"Adding or removing a node remaps only about K/n keys instead of nearly all of them; virtual nodes improve balance.\"\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "**Consistent hashing** — a distributed hashing scheme that maps keys and nodes onto a ring, assigning each key to the next node clockwise[1]. Adding or removing a node remaps only about K/n keys rather than nearly all of them[2]. Virtual nodes may be used to improve balance[2].\n\n---\n**Sources**\n[1] Source 1: “Consistent hashing maps keys and nodes onto a ring; a key is owned by the next node clockwise.”\n[2] Source 2: “Adding or removing a node remaps only about K/n keys instead of nearly all of them; virtual nodes improve balance.”\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "# Consistent hashing\n\n**Consistent hashing** is a distributed hashing scheme in which keys and nodes (servers, storage units, etc.) are mapped onto a ring, and each key is owned by the next node encountered moving clockwise [1].\n\n## Properties\n- **Minimal remapping on topology change**: adding or removing a node remaps only about $K/n$ keys (where $K$ is the total key count and $n$ the number of nodes), rather than nearly all keys [2].\n- **Balance**: virtual nodes improve load balance among nodes [2].\n\n## References\n[1] Source 1 — ring mapping; clockwise ownership.\n[2] Source 2 — remap scope ~K/n; virtual nodes improve balance.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "# Consistent hashing\n\nConsistent hashing maps keys and nodes onto a ring; a key is owned by the next node clockwise [1].\n\nAdding or removing a node remaps only about `K/n` keys instead of nearly all of them; virtual nodes improve balance [2].\n\n**Sources:**\n1. Consistent hashing maps keys and nodes onto a ring; a key is owned by the next node clockwise.\n2. Adding or removing a node remaps only about K/n keys instead of nearly all of them; virtual nodes improve balance.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# Consistent Hashing\n\n**Consistent hashing** is a distributed hashing scheme that maps both keys and nodes onto a ring structure, where each key is assigned to the next node encountered clockwise on the ring.[1]\n\nThe technique minimizes redistribution when the node set changes: adding or removing a node remaps approximately K/n keys (where K is the total number of keys and n is the number of nodes), rather than remapping nearly all keys as in traditional hashing.[2] Virtual nodes—multiple ring positions per physical node—are used to improve load balance across the system.[2]\n\n## References\n[1] SOURCE 1  \n[2] SOURCE 2\n"}, "extract-andy-microsandbox" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "No tools needed — the NOTE is inline. Concept inventory:\n\n**Canonical (established industry terms)**\n\n- **microVM** — lightweight VM engineered for container-class startup; coined by AWS's Firecracker (2018), now a standard industry term (also Cloud Hypervisor, QEMU microvm). Canonical *via coinage that stuck*.\n- **OCI image** — Open Container Initiative image specification; the standard, portable container image format. Fully canonical.\n- **Nix-built OCI images** — using the Nix package manager / Nixpkgs to deterministically build OCI images (`dockerTools.buildImage` and successors). Established practice, canonical.\n- **Hardware-virtualization isolation** — VMs isolated by CPU/memory virtualization (VT-x/AMD-V, KVM), vs. container namespaces/cgroups sharing one kernel. Canonical distinction in the sandboxing literature.\n- **Ephemeral / disposable machine** — boot-fresh, destroy-after-use VMs; Firecracker's original Lambda/Fargate design and the standard \"temporary sandbox\" pattern. Canonical concept.\n- **Infrastructure as code** — \"script the whole VM launch as code\": declarative/programmatic environment provisioning. Canonical term and practice.\n- **Per-workload isolation (\"each agent gets its own\")** — one isolated execution environment per tenant/process; the classic multi-tenancy security principle (same idea as per-user VMs, qubes-style compartmentalization).\n\n**Author's coinage / framing**\n\n- **microsandboxing** — not an established industry term; it's the author's label for the *combination*: microVM + OCI-image supply chain + ephemeral lifecycle, applied to sandboxing AI agents. The underlying pieces are all canonical; the packaging under this name is the coinage.\n- **\"agent\" as the sandboxed tenant** — the concepts are old; applying them to AI coding agents is the recent framing, not a new mechanism.\n\n**Summary:** the NOTE invokes no novel mechanism — microVM, OCI, Nix, KVM-backed isolation, ephemerality, and IaC are all well-established. The only coined element is the umbrella term *microsandboxing* and its application to agent workloads.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "**Canonical**\n\n- **MicroVM** — lightweight guest using minimal device model (Firecracker, Cloud Hypervisor).\n- **OCI image** — Open Container Initiative standard packaging format.\n- **Nix** — purely functional package manager and build system.\n- **Container** — OS-level process/virtualization isolation.\n- **Hardware virtualization** — CPU-enforced guest boundary (VT-x/AMD-V).\n- **Virtual Machine (VM)** — kernel + userspace guest environment.\n- **Ephemeral / throwaway infrastructure** — single-use, immutable compute; provision, run, discard.\n- **Infrastructure as Code** — programmatic, reproducible launch/configuration.\n\n**Author’s coinage**\n\n- **Microsandboxing** — the umbrella term for the specific workflow: per-agent, sub-second, Nix-built, OCI-booted microVMs that are discarded after the agent run.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "No NOTE file exists in-repo; treating the inline NOTE as the source.\n\n## Concepts the NOTE invokes, canonical vs coinage\n\n**Canonical terms/concepts:**\n1. **MicroVM** — canonical. Term AWS coined for Firecracker (2018); now generic across Kata Containers, Cloud Hypervisor. NOTE's meaning matches the standard one: minimal-footprint VM designed to run a single workload.\n2. **OCI image** — canonical. Open Container Initiative image format spec; the standard unit of packaged container software.\n3. **Nix → OCI image pipeline** — canonical. Building OCI images reproducibly with Nix (`nix2container`, `dockerTools`) is an established practice.\n4. **Booting a microVM from a container image** — canonical pattern in that ecosystem (Ignite, Kata Containers: OCI image as guest rootfs). Implicitly also invokes the container concept itself.\n5. **Hardware-virtualization isolation** — canonical. Hypervisor/KVM-enforced isolation as distinguished from shared-kernel container isolation; \"isolated like a VM\" is the canonical microVM claim.\n6. **Container-like startup** — canonical microVM performance property: Firecracker advertises ~100 ms-class boot. \"Well under a second\" is the standard microVM elevator pitch, not the author's framing.\n7. **Per-agent sandbox** — canonical in LLM-agent infrastructure. One sandbox per workload/agent is the dominant design (E2B, Modal, gVisor/Kata-based code executors). NOTE maps this onto microVMs.\n8. **Infrastructure as code** — canonical. \"Script the whole VM launch as code\" is IaC/declarative provisioning applied to a single VM lifecycle.\n9. **Ephemeral/disposable compute** — canonical. \"Run the agent, throw the machine away\" is the disposable/immutable-infrastructure pattern (\"cattle, not pets\"), adopted per workload.\n\n**Coinage / author framing:**\n1. **\"microsandboxing\"** — the author's umbrella term. No standard definition in the OCI/CNCF/Firecracker spec literature; it is informal shorthand for sandboxing-by-microVM. The underlying pattern is canonical; the word is not.\n\n**Engineer's note:** \"boots from an OCI image\" is slightly inexact as stated — an OCI image contains no kernel, so a microVM boot needs an injected kernel plus rootfs conversion. Established practice, but the paraphrase papers over that step.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "Underlying concepts, each tagged:\n\n**Canonical (well-established)**\n\n- **microVM** — standard term; Firecracker, QEMU `microvm` machine type, Kata Containers.\n- **OCI image** — Open Container Initiative spec, canonical.\n- **Reproducible image built with Nix** — nixpkgs `dockerTools`/`streamLayeredImage`; canonical pattern.\n- **Hardware-virtualization isolation** — VMM/hypervisor isolation (vs. shared-kernel containers); canonical.\n- **Sub-second cold start** — the defining Firecracker-derived property; canonical metric.\n- **Per-tenant/per-agent VM isolation** — one workload per VM, not shared; canonical multitenancy practice.\n- **Infrastructure-as-Code / launching the VM as scripted code** — canonical.\n- **Ephemeral, disposable machine (\"throw it away\")** — cattle-not-pets, serverless/Fargate-style ephemeral compute; canonical.\n\n**Author's coinage**\n\n- **microsandboxing** — portmanteau of *microVM* + *sandboxing*; not an established term of art. The mechanism (Firecracker-style microVM) is canonical; the framing name is Andy's.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "**Canonical concepts:**\n- **microVM** — lightweight virtual machines (Firecracker, Cloud Hypervisor, etc.)\n- **OCI image** — Open Container Initiative image format\n- **Nix** — declarative package manager and NixOS build system\n- **container** — OS-level virtualization (Docker, containerd, etc.)\n- **hardware virtualization** — CPU-assisted isolation (Intel VT-x, AMD-V, ARM Virtualization Extensions)\n- **VM (virtual machine)** — full machine emulation/virtualization\n- **infrastructure as code** — scripted/declarative system provisioning\n- **ephemeral compute** — disposable, stateless execution instances\n- **process isolation** — resource and security boundaries between workloads\n- **agent** — autonomous compute process/service\n\n**Author's coinage:**\n- **\"microsandboxing\"** — Andy Smith's term for this specific pattern: per-agent microVM isolation with sub-second boot from declarative OCI images, combined with ephemeral lifecycle (boot → execute → destroy)\n\nThe note synthesizes existing primitives (microVMs, OCI, Nix, hardware isolation) into a named pattern emphasizing the \"micro\" granularity (per-agent, sub-second) and sandboxing guarantee (hardware-isolated, immutable base, no shared state across runs).\n"}, "connect-idempotency-conflation" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "**Flagged: the note misinterprets in both directions.**\n\n**Connection (corrected):** Idempotency = retrying the same request yields the same effect as sending it once. It neither implies nor is implied by thread-safety (correct behavior under concurrent access) or atomicity (all-or-nothing execution). The three are orthogonal; an idempotent endpoint can still race on shared state and still fail mid-way leaving partial effects.\n\n**Two errors in the claim:**\n\n1. **\"Unique request ID\" defeats idempotency.** An idempotency key must be *client-supplied and stable across retries* of the same logical operation. A fresh unique ID per request makes each retry look like a new request — duplicates produce duplicate side effects. Correct design: client sends one key per logical operation; server stores `key → result` and returns the stored response on repeats.\n\n2. **The \"so now\" inference is backwards.** Making idempotency actually hold *requires* thread-safety and atomicity, not the reverse: concurrent requests with the same key need an atomic check-and-set (e.g., unique constraint on the key row, or lock) or both may execute; partial failure needs transactional boundaries or the stored \"result\" may be inconsistent. Idempotency is the *consequence* of those mechanisms plus key dedup — it grants neither for free.\n\n**Corrected claim:** \"We added a client-supplied idempotency key with atomic dedup (unique-key insert or lock), which makes retries safe. Thread-safety and atomicity of the key store are what *enable* the idempotency; they remain separate invariants that must be verified independently.\"\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "The note misinterprets idempotency as sufficient for thread-safety and atomicity.\n\nA unique request ID enables **idempotency** by letting the server deduplicate retries, but it does not prevent two threads from processing the same ID concurrently. **Thread-safety** still requires explicit synchronization (e.g., locks, conditional DB writes) against that race. **Atomicity** still requires transactional boundaries so the operation completes entirely or not at all; a partially failed idempotent request can still leave inconsistent state.\n\nThese are orthogonal concerns: the request ID guards against duplicate *effect*, not against concurrent *races* or partial *failures*.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "Connection: Idempotency means executing a request twice yields the same state as once. A unique request ID alone doesn't confer that — it's only a key for detecting duplicates. What actually makes the endpoint idempotent is server-side dedup: record the ID (e.g., unique constraint) before applying the effect, and replay a request reusing that ID returns the original result instead of re-applying.\n\nMisinterpretations to flag:\n- Idempotency ≠ thread-safety ≠ atomicity. The note implies the ID gives all three — a non sequitur.\n- \"Unique\" per request kills dedup: if retries mint new IDs, the same semantic request executes repeatedly. The client must reuse one ID per logical request.\n- Thread-safety isn't automatic either: only if the check-and-record is itself atomic (single compare-and-set on the ID) do concurrent duplicates collapse to one execution. That's a property of the dedup store, not of generating an ID.\n\nCorrect restatement: \"We added a dedup ledger keyed by a client-supplied request ID to make repeated requests idempotent; with an atomic insert on the ID, it also holds under concurrency.\"\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "The connection is correct on idempotency — repeated identical requests have the same effect as one — but the jump to “therefore thread-safe and atomic” is wrong.\n\n- A unique request ID can act as an **idempotency key** to detect/route retries, but it doesn’t synchronize concurrent requests.\n- **Thread-safe** means safe under concurrent access to shared state.\n- **Atomic** means an operation is all-or-nothing.\n\nCorrect version: the ID makes the endpoint **idempotent**; to also be safe under concurrency, the dedupe check and effect must be atomic (e.g. transactional insert-on-conflict or a lock), and shared state still needs proper synchronization.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "**Connection**: Idempotency (same request → same result) ≠ thread-safety (concurrent access safety) ≠ atomicity (all-or-nothing execution).\n\n**Correction**: The claim **conflates distinct properties**. A unique request ID enables **idempotency** by detecting duplicates. It does NOT provide:\n- **Thread-safety**: Two different request IDs can still race on shared state (requires locks/synchronization)\n- **Atomicity**: The operation can still fail partially or expose intermediate states (requires transactions/CAS)\n\nIdempotency prevents duplicate work; thread-safety prevents concurrent corruption; atomicity prevents partial failures. Independent concerns requiring different mechanisms.\n"}, "synth-linearizability" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "**Linearizability**\n\nLinearizability is a strong consistency model in which each operation appears to take effect atomically at some instant between its invocation and response [1]. It gives the illusion of a single copy of the data and preserves real-time ordering of operations [2].\n\n**Sources**\n1. Source 1 (definition of linearizability).\n2. Source 2 (single-copy illusion and real-time ordering).\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "**Linearizability**\n\nA strong consistency model in which each operation appears to take effect atomically at some instant between its invocation and response.[1] It gives the illusion of a single copy of the data and preserves real-time ordering of operations.[2]\n\n---\n\n**Sources**\n\n[1] Source 1  \n[2] Source 2\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "# Linearizability\n\n**Linearizability** is a strong consistency model [S1] in which each operation appears to take effect atomically at some instant between its invocation and its response [S1]. It provides the illusion of a single copy of the data [S2] and preserves the real-time ordering of operations [S2].\n\n## Key characteristics\n- **Atomicity:** Every operation is perceived to occur at a single point in time [S1].\n- **Interval constraint:** That point falls within the interval from the operation's invocation to its response [S1].\n- **Single-copy illusion:** The system behaves as if it maintains one copy of the data [S2].\n- **Real-time ordering:** If one operation completes before another begins, the observed order reflects that [S2].\n\n## Citations\n- S1: \"Linearizability is a strong consistency model in which each operation appears to take effect atomically at some instant between its invocation and response.\"\n- S2: \"It gives the illusion of a single copy of the data and preserves real-time ordering of operations.\"\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "**Linearizability**\n\nA strong consistency model in which each operation appears to take effect atomically at some instant between its invocation and response [1]. It gives the illusion of a single copy of the data and preserves real-time ordering of operations [2].\n\nReferences:\n[1] Source 1.\n[2] Source 2.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# Linearizability\n\n**Linearizability** is a strong consistency model in distributed computing and concurrent systems in which each operation appears to take effect atomically at some instant between its invocation and response.[1] The model gives the illusion of a single copy of the data and preserves real-time ordering of operations.[2]\n\n## Properties\n\n- **Atomic visibility**: Operations take effect instantaneously at a point between invocation and completion[1]\n- **Single-copy semantics**: System behaves as if only one copy of the data exists[2]  \n- **Real-time ordering**: Preserves the temporal order in which operations occur[2]\n\n---\n**References**  \n[1] SOURCE 1  \n[2] SOURCE 2\n"}, "connect-consistent-hashing-correct" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "**Confirmed.** The note matches the concept.\n\n- Consistent hashing places nodes and keys on a ring; a key lives on the first node clockwise from its position. When a node joins, it takes over only the arc it occupies — on average **~K/n** of K keys move to the new node. When it leaves, only its ~K/n keys move to successors.\n- Contrast with naive `hash(key) mod n`: a change in n changes the modulus, remapping **~K(1 − 1/n)** keys — nearly everything.\n- \"Small fraction instead of remapping everything\" is exactly the ~K/n vs ~K distinction, so the note is accurate.\n\nOne caveat worth adding if precision matters: ~K/n holds *on average* and assumes enough virtual nodes per physical node; with a bare ring (no replicas/vnodes) the moved fraction can deviate significantly from K/n due to uneven arc lengths.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "Confirm. Consistent hashing places nodes and keys on the same ring; each node serves the keys in its clockwise interval. Adding a node inserts one new point on the ring and splits exactly one existing interval, so only the keys in that interval—expected fraction 1/n of total K, i.e. ~K/n—migrate to the new node. The rest stay put. Traditional modulo hashing (`key % n`) remaps ~K·n/(n+1) keys because the denominator changes for every key.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "Confirmed, with the usual caveat.\n\nWith $N$ nodes and $K$ keys on a ring, one node's arc holds ~$K/N$ keys in expectation. A join/leave re-homes exactly those keys, so ~$K/N$ keys move — a $1/N$ fraction. That matches the note.\n\nCaveat: \"small\" means *relative to $N$*, not a fixed tiny amount: if $N$ is small (say 2), half the keys remap, which is exactly what naive remapping does at the same scale. The win is that the fraction *shrinks toward 0 as $N$ grows*, whereas naive rehashing (e.g. `key % n`) remaps ~$K - K/N$ keys.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "<connection>\nConfirm. Consistent hashing bounds remapping to ~K/n keys — a 1/n fraction. Adding a node splits one neighbor's ring range and absorbs ~K/n keys; removing a node redistributes only its own ~K/n range. Contrast: naive `hash(key) mod n` with n→n+1 remaps ~K·n/(n+1) ≈ nearly all keys. \"Small fraction\" = ~1/n, not \"everything.\" Correct as stated, assuming uniformly distributed keys (virtual nodes make this hold in practice).\n</connection>\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "**Connection: Confirmed**\n\nClaim matches concept. Traditional hashing (`hash(key) % n`) remaps ~K keys when n changes—nearly everything. Consistent hashing remaps only the ~K/n keys that would have hashed to adjacent nodes' now-shifted ranges—exactly \"a small fraction instead of everything.\"\n\n**Quantitative check**: n=10 → adding 1 node moves ~K/11 ≈ 9% of keys vs. ~91% with modulo hashing.\n"}, "extract-mundane-restraint" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "The NOTE invokes almost no concepts worth listing. What it does invoke:\n\n- **Git / push to `main`** — canonical. Version control, remote branch push, `main` as default branch name.\n- **README** — canonical convention (file name, not a formal spec).\n\nThat's it. \"Renamed project folder,\" \"fixed a typo,\" \"project\" are ordinary language, not established technical concepts. No coinages present.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "- project folder — canonical\n- README — canonical\n- push (Git) — canonical\n- `main` branch — canonical\n\nNo coinages invoked.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "It invokes essentially none. The NOTE is a plain-language to-do list: folder rename, README typo fix, push.\n\nMarginal, ordinary-language references only (not genuinely *invoked* as concepts):\n\n- \"pushed to main\" — Git; specifically the default branch name and push operation. Canonical (version control convention).\n- \"README\" — conventional documentation filename. Canonical (file-naming convention).\n\nNo coinage, no technical machinery engaged. Those two are incidental nouns, not activated concepts — I would not count them as invocations either. Net: few-to-none; do not read further into it.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "- `README` as the conventional project documentation entry file — **canonical** (de facto project convention).\n- `push` to a remote `main` branch — **canonical** (Git/version-control terminology).\n- Renaming the project folder and fixing a typo — plain filesystem/editorial actions, **not established technical concepts**.\n\nNo coinage.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "**Few well-established concepts; no inflation warranted:**\n\n1. **Git version control** (canonical) — \"pushed to main\"\n2. **README convention** (canonical) — documentation file naming\n\nThe note describes routine project maintenance: filesystem rename, typo fix, git push. No architectural patterns, specialized techniques, or domain concepts invoked.\n"}, "connect-misinterpretation" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "**Correction — the claim misinterprets CAP.** Partition tolerance is not a property you can \"drop\": networks will partition whether you like it or not. CAP says that *during* a partition, a system must choose between consistency (C) and availability (A); it says nothing about choosing P. \"Chose CA\" describes a single-node system, not a distributed one.\n\n**Connection:** The note conflates \"we tolerate partitions poorly\" with \"we opted out of partitions.\" The correct framing: under partition, this system favors A over C (or vice versa). Rephrase as, e.g., \"during partitions we sacrifice consistency for availability (AP)\" — or state which side it actually takes.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "The claim misinterprets the theorem.\n\n**Correction:** Partition tolerance is not a feature you can simply drop in a real distributed system—network partitions are a physical reality. The CAP theorem states that *when* a partition occurs, the system must choose between consistency (C) and availability (A). Claiming a distributed system is \"CA\" means it assumes partitions will never happen, which is operationally invalid. The meaningful design choice is **CP** or **AP**.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "Flag: the claim misinterprets CAP.\n\nCAP's partition tolerance isn't a feature you select or drop — it's a property of the environment. Network partitions occur whether you choose to \"tolerate\" them or not (Gilbert & Lynch's proof is conditional: *if* a partition occurs). So the real choice during a partition is between consistency (C) and availability (A); P is the precondition that forces that choice, not a third option you can trade away.\n\n\"CA\" only describes behavior *in the absence* of a partition: a CA-labeled system can be consistent and available while the network is healthy. The moment a partition happens, it must degrade to either C or A — systems that fail or refuse requests are choosing C over A; systems that serve possibly stale data are choosing A over C. A system that \"chose CA\" has simply not specified what it does under partition, which is the actual design decision.\n\nCorrection: \"We chose consistency over availability during partitions\" — that is `CP`, the honest reading. If the intent was \"consistent and available when the network is intact, and we accept downtime when partitioned,\" say that directly; it's equivalent to CP. `P` cannot be dropped; it can only be acknowledged.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "The note misstates CAP. Partition tolerance isn't a tunable you can drop; partitions will happen, so a real distributed system must tolerate them. The choice is only **C vs A during a partition** — hence systems are described as **CP** or **AP**, not \"CA.\" \"CA\" is possible only if you assume no partitions (e.g., a single node or a network that never fails), which is exactly the unrealistic case CAP excludes. So \"we chose CA and dropped P\" conflates a configuration choice with a physical condition you can't opt out of.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "**Correction needed.** The claim misinterprets CAP.\n\n**The error:** \"Dropping partition tolerance\" implies partitions are optional. They aren't—network failures occur whether you design for them or not.\n\n**What CAP actually means:** During a partition (which *will* happen), choose either:\n- **CP**: Reject requests to preserve consistency\n- **AP**: Accept requests risking inconsistency\n\n**CA systems don't exist** in distributed architectures because partitions are inevitable. A \"CA\" design is just a system that hasn't decided what to do when partitions occur—it will fail unpredictably.\n\n**Correct framing:** \"We chose CP\" (consistency over availability during partitions) or \"We chose AP\" (availability over consistency during partitions).\n"}, "synth-merkle-tree" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "# Merkle tree\n\n**Merkle tree** — a hash tree in which every leaf is the hash of a data block and every internal node is the hash of its children [1].\n\n## Properties\n\n- **Commitment**: the root hash commits to all data in the tree [2].\n- **Integrity**: any tampering with the underlying data changes the root hash [2].\n- **Comparison**: two trees can be compared top-down to locate differences in logarithmic work [2].\n\n## References\n\n1. Source 1 — definition of leaf and internal node structure.\n2. Source 2 — root-hash commitment, top-down comparison, tamper sensitivity.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "**Merkle tree**\n\nA tree in which every leaf is the hash of a data block and every internal node is the hash of its children.[1] The root hash commits to all data; two trees can be compared top-down to locate differences in logarithmic work, and any tampering changes the root.[2]\n\n---\n**Sources**\n\n[1] *Source 1*  \n[2] *Source 2*\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "Merkle tree — a tree in which every leaf is the hash of a data block and every internal node is the hash of its children.[1]\n\nThe root hash commits to all data.[2] Two trees can be compared top-down to locate differences in logarithmic work.[2] Any tampering changes the root.[2]\n\n1. Src 1 — tree structure definition.\n2. Src 2 — root commitment, logarithmic comparison, tamper detection.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "**Merkle tree** — a tree in which every leaf is the hash of a data block and every internal node is the hash of its children [1]. The root hash commits to all data; two trees can be compared top-down to locate differences in logarithmic work, and any tampering changes the root [2].\n\n[1] Source 1  \n[2] Source 2\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# Merkle Tree\n\nA **Merkle tree** is a tree data structure in which every leaf node contains the cryptographic hash of a data block, and every internal node contains the hash of its children.[1] The root hash cryptographically commits to all data in the tree.[2]\n\n## Properties\n\nTwo Merkle trees can be compared top-down to locate differences in logarithmic work.[2] Any tampering with the underlying data causes the root hash to change, providing tamper-evidence.[2]\n\n## References\n\n[1] SOURCE 1  \n[2] SOURCE 2\n"}, "connect-correct" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "**Confirmed.** The claim applies idempotency correctly.\n\nA payment is not inherently idempotent — a naive retry re-executes the charge, so `charge(x)` twice = two charges. The claim's \"unless\" clause is exactly the concept: with idempotency (e.g., an idempotency key identifying the original request), the server recognizes the retry as the same operation and returns the original result, so `charge(x)` repeated has the same effect as `charge(x)` once. No misinterpretation to correct.\n\nOne refinement worth noting: the risk stated is precise — a timeout is ambiguous (the charge may or may not have gone through), which is why retries *without* idempotency can double-charge; the fix is making the operation idempotent, not avoiding retries.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "The claim is correct.  \n\nWithout idempotency, a retry after a timeout is indistinguishable from a new request, so the server may process both and charge the customer twice. An idempotent operation—typically enforced with an idempotency key—ensures duplicate requests produce the same effect as a single request, preventing double-charging.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "Confirmed. The claim is a direct application of the definition.\n\n- Non-idempotent retry: the retry is a *new* operation. If the timed-out first attempt actually succeeded, the retry creates a second charge — the effect of two operations differs from one.\n- Idempotent retry: the retry (same request key) yields the same effect as the first attempt — a single charge — regardless of whether the first attempt landed.\n\nNo misinterpretation. The claim's uncertainty (\"could\") is accurate: the risk exists only when the timeout left the first attempt's outcome unknown, which is exactly the case idempotency resolves.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "Confirmed.\n\nA timeout leaves the client unsure whether the first request was processed. A retry then reaches the server a second time. If the payment endpoint is idempotent — typically via an idempotency key — the server recognizes the repeat and returns the original result instead of executing a second charge. The operation therefore has the same effect once or many times, matching the concept exactly. Without it, the second execution can create a second charge.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "**Connection:** Confirms. Without idempotency, each retry is treated as a new payment → customer charged N times for N attempts. With idempotency (e.g., via request ID), all retries of the same logical payment produce the same outcome—one charge—regardless of how many times the client sends the request. The operation \"create payment for request X\" has the same effect whether executed once or repeated.\n"}, "extract-andy-ephemeral" {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" "**Explicitly named concepts (invoked by term):**\n\n| Concept | Status | Invoking phrase |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sandboxing | Canonical (security mechanism, standard term since ≥ Java 1.0) | \"runs in a sandbox prepared specifically for it\" |\n| Principle of least privilege | Canonical (Saltzer & Schroeder, 1975) | \"least privilege is the first thing you have to build\" |\n| Reproducible environments | Canonical (reproducible builds, deterministic/Nix-style environments) | \"Environments are reproducible\" |\n| Immutability | Canonical (immutable infrastructure / immutable data) | \"immutable for the duration of a tick\" |\n| Tick | Canonical term in discrete-time simulation and game/event loops; its application to agent execution is a borrowing, not an established convention | \"for the duration of a tick\" |\n\n**Invoked by mechanism, not by name — these are paraphrases of canonical ideas, not named in the text:**\n\n| Concept | Status | Invoking phrase |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"Physical access ⇒ compromise\" tenet | Canonical security axiom (folklore-level, widely held, not a formal theory) | \"An agent with physical access to secrets will get at them sooner or later\" |\n| No self-authorization / delegated authority | Canonical (object-capability model; RBAC constraints) | \"can request more rights but cannot grant them to itself\" |\n| Explicit/whole-state design (statelessness + snapshot-ability) | [INFERENCE] Evokes the canonical pattern in containerization/actor systems (all state externalized, hence checkpointable), but the note names no concept here | \"its whole state is the sandbox plus a working directory\" |\n\n**Coinage:**\n\n- **\"Ephemeral agents\"** — author's own label. It rides on the canonical \"ephemeral X\" pattern (ephemeral containers, ephemeral environments), but \"ephemeral agents\" is not an established term.\n\n**Not concepts invoked:** \"secrets,\" \"working directory,\" \"rights\" — ordinary vocabulary, not theory terms.\n\nCaveat: the middle table's attributions are my reading of mechanism, not claims the author named those concepts; only the first table's items are invoked by their established names.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "- **Sandbox / sandboxing** — **CANONICAL established** — “sandbox prepared specifically for it”\n- **Principle of Least Privilege** — **CANONICAL established** — “least privilege is the first thing you have to build”\n- **Reproducible environments** (or reproducible builds) — **CANONICAL established** — “Environments are reproducible”\n- **Immutable infrastructure / immutable environments** — **CANONICAL established** — “immutable for the duration of a tick”\n- **Physical-access compromise maxim** (physical access defeats secrecy) — **CANONICAL established** — “An agent with physical access to secrets will get at them sooner or later”\n- **Tick** (as the fixed execution epoch) — **Author’s own** (model-specific term) — “for the duration of a tick”\n- **Ephemeral agents** — **Author’s own** — “’Ephemeral agents’”\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "Concepts invoked, with the phrase that invokes each:\n\n| Concept | Status | Invoking phrase |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Sandboxing** (run untrusted code in an isolated, restricted environment) | CANONICAL (long-standing OS/security isolation technique) | \"runs in a sandbox prepared specifically for it\" |\n| **Physical access ⇒ full compromise** (software access control cannot protect secrets from a determined physically present party; \"physical access is root access\") | CANONICAL security adage | \"An agent with physical access to secrets will get at them sooner or later\" |\n| **Least privilege** (Saltzer & Schroeder, 1975) | CANONICAL | \"least privilege is the first thing you have to build\" |\n| **Reproducibility** of environments/builds | CANONICAL (e.g., reproducible-builds practice) | \"Environments are reproducible\" |\n| **Immutability / immutable infrastructure** | CANONICAL (deployment pattern: no in-place mutation; recreate instead) | \"immutable for the duration of a tick\" |\n| **No self-authorized privilege escalation** (rights must be granted by an authority that already holds them; a subject cannot unilaterally amplify its own authority) | Established property with no single canonical name; phrasing is the author's | \"an agent can request more rights but cannot grant them to itself\" |\n| **Statelessness / externalized state** (worker's state lives only in its environment, not in the agent) | CANONICAL distributed-systems pattern | \"its whole state is the sandbox plus a working directory\" |\n\nGray areas, flagged, not asserted as canonical:\n- **\"tick\"** — borrowed jargon (time-slice from scheduling/game loops), but its use here (an immutable per-agent epoch) is the author's repurposing, not a stand-alone established concept.\n- **\"ephemeral agents\"** — the author's own coinage for the architecture; \"ephemeral\" environments are an established idea, but this pairing is not.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "- **Sandboxing** — CANONICAL established concept — “Each agent runs in a sandbox prepared specifically for it.”\n- **Physical access ⇒ eventual compromise** — CANONICAL security maxim — “An agent with physical access to secrets will get at them sooner or later.”\n- **Least privilege** — CANONICAL established principle (Saltzer & Schroeder) — “least privilege is the first thing you have to build.”\n- **Reproducible environments** — CANONICAL established practice (reproducible builds/infrastructure) — “Environments are reproducible.”\n- **Immutability / immutable infrastructure** — CANONICAL established practice — “and immutable for the duration of a tick.”\n- **Privilege separation / no self-grant of rights** — CANONICAL established security technique — “an agent can request more rights but cannot grant them to itself.”\n- **Total agent state = sandbox + working directory** — Author’s own operational definition, not a canonical named concept — “its whole state is the sandbox plus a working directory.”\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# Well-Established Concepts Invoked\n\n1. **Sandbox**\n   - Status: CANONICAL\n   - Phrase: \"Each agent runs in a sandbox prepared specifically for it\"\n\n2. **Least Privilege**\n   - Status: CANONICAL\n   - Phrase: \"so least privilege is the first thing you have to build\"\n\n3. **Physical Access (security axiom)**\n   - Status: CANONICAL\n   - Phrase: \"An agent with physical access to secrets will get at them sooner or later\"\n\n4. **Immutability**\n   - Status: CANONICAL\n   - Phrase: \"Environments are reproducible and immutable for the duration of a tick\"\n\n5. **Reproducible Environments**\n   - Status: CANONICAL\n   - Phrase: \"Environments are reproducible and immutable\"\n\n6. **Privilege Escalation Prevention**\n   - Status: CANONICAL\n   - Phrase: \"an agent can request more rights but cannot grant them to itself\"\n"}}, :usage {"opencode-go/glm-5.2" {:provider "opencode-go", :delta 0}, "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" {:provider "opencode-go", :delta 1}, "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" {:provider "opencode-go", :delta 0}, "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" {:provider "opencode-go", :delta 6}, "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" {:provider "anthropic", :delta 0}}}